kubectl edit, scale, set

commands are run once commands, you will not know who created it and modified, hard to keep track of whoc

managing objects with configuration manifests files

kubectl create -f ngnix.yaml

we can now have ngnix.yaml files always to track it
we can put it in code repository and put together a change review and approval process around these files
so that a PR is reviewed and approved before appled to production environment

update objects
1) kubectl edit pod my-pod
it will edit live objects without accessing or saving changes to the configuration file
bypassing the review and approval

in future if new change is applied using edit or using replace, the previous change is lost accordingly the differnce b/w live and current changes required

2)kubectl replace -f ngnix.yaml
a better option would be updating the configuration file with required changes and running the 
kubectl replace -f ngnix.yaml to update the objects

changes made are recorded and tracked

to completely delete the objects and recreate as per new version of configuration file
kubectl replace --force -f ngnix.yaml

